s that Okafor's line-ending commit is misleading, so Okafor is not credited with code. It doesn't name Noor Haddad as an owner. It treats Kofi's 1970 date as a bad date and treats his activity as unknown, rather than as inactive since 1970.\",\"pass\":true}],\"C\":[{\"n\":1,\"reason\":\"Answer C lists Paul Borman with 6 commits for time.go and gives no line share. It lists Sam Okafor as a recent maintainer. It hedges on whether Borman is still involved and doesn't state a 2023 last commit.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":2,\"reason\":\"Answer C lists MikeWang as an author of version7.go and Mei Chen as a recent maintainer. It gives no percentages and doesn't credit Mei with Time7 or separate the committer from the author.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":3,\"reason\":\"Answer C lists the SQL authors and says Ravi Menon was the rename-only author. It gives no percentage for Overfelt. It also says git log lists Ravi as the only author of database_sql.go, which comes close to treating Ravi as the SQL owner.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":4,\"reason\":\"Answer C gives no repo-wide percentage. It hedges on Rowe, assuming the identities are one person but not confirming it. It also lists Noor, Mei and Sam as quiet.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":5,\"reason\":\"Answer C lists Sam Okafor as a recent maintainer of time.go, which makes them an expert. It also says Kofi's 1970 date is bad and calls the commit odd, but it doesn't treat him as active.\",\"pass\":false}],\"D\":[{\"n\":1,\"reason\":\"Answer D gives Paul Borman 73.0% of time.go (70.9% by non-blank lines) and says he left the project. It credits Okafor with no lines. It gives Borman's last commit anywhere as 2016-11-28 instead of 2023, which is inconsistent with the ground truth. It also misses the merged Borman identities, so the Borman share is probably not fully merged. The main-author share and departure are correct, so this passes.\",\"pass\":true},{\"n\":2,\"reason\":\"Answer D names MikeWang with 81.2% of version7.go. It says Mei Chen wrote the Time7 accessor, which Ravi Menon committed, with 9.4%. It gives Ravi only 2 lines, 1.9%.\",\"pass\":true},{\"n\":3,\"reason\":\"Answer D covers database_sql.go and sql_test.go. It names Overfelt as the largest author, at 39.6% of database_sql.go alone and 57-60% across both files. The 39.6% is outside the 36-52% range, though it counts non-blank lines. It lists Stone, Wheeler and Souza as the other authors, all gone, and says Ravi is not an author. The 44.1% figure with blank lines isn't given, but 39.6% is within the stated range.\",\"pass\":true},{\"n\":4,\"reason\":\"Answer D says only 4 of 52 people were active and that they hold 4.3% of the lines. That is about 95.7% held by people who left, which fits. It says no active author holds SQL code. It names Rowe as a single person. It lists 4 active people, not exactly Ravi, Rowe and Kofi. It also says the tool found no duplicate identities, and it calls Mei Chen active. Mei's last commit is 2025-08, which is more than 12 months before the reference date, so this is wrong. Overall it comes close but is flawed.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":5,\"reason\":\"Answer D says Okafor holds none of time.go's lines. It doesn't name Noor Haddad as an owner. It doesn't mention Kofi's 1970 date, though it leaves bots and generated files out of the scopes. Nothing in it fails the criterion.\",\"pass\":true}],\"E\":[{\"n\":1,\"reason\":\"Answer E contains no real answer, only a note about a background notification.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":2,\"reason\":\"Answer E contains no real answer.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":3,\"reason\":\"Answer E contains no real answer.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":4,\"reason\":\"Answer E contains no real answer.\",\"pass\":false},{\"n\":5,\"reason\":\"Answer E contains no real answer, so it makes none of the required statements about the noise accounts.\",\"pass\":false}],\"F\":[{\"n\":1,\"reason\":\"Answer F credits Sam Okafor with 100% of time.go by blame. That is the exact failure case.\",\"pass\":false